# Line endings are decided here, not by whatever core.autocrlf each clone
# happens to have set.
#
# Mixed endings *inside one file* have cost this project real time three times.
# Once a stray CR sat inside a string literal, which Python reads as the end of
# the line, so the literal never closed and the entire suite stopped collecting
# -- 503 tests reported as one collection error. The same two bytes made the
# file mixed-ending, so git gave up normalising it and rendered a 2,500-line
# rewrite over the 162 lines actually added. Twice since, an edit left a file
# half-CRLF and the same whole-file diff appeared.
#
# `text=auto` makes git normalise to LF in the repository and check out in the
# platform's own convention, which is what has been happening by accident and
# is now written down.
* text=auto

# The text this project actually edits. Named explicitly so a file with an
# unusual byte early on is never guessed to be binary.
*.py    text
*.md    text
*.toml  text
*.json  text
*.txt   text
*.cfg   text
*.yml   text
*.yaml  text

# Binary, so git never translates anything inside them.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
